---
/**
 * CommerceSettingsSaveBar — a sticky save/reset bar for a commerce settings
 * form (Issue #244), ported from `media-lenterakalteng`'s
 * `SettingsSaveBar.astro` (that fork's ADR-0124) into this module's own
 * `src/components/` — never `src/pages/admin/`, see
 * `CommerceMarketingTabs.astro`'s own docblock for why a shared partial
 * placed under the admin route tree reads as an unregistered screen to
 * `access-chokepoint-check.ts`.
 *
 * ## What this component is
 *
 * A **presentation-only** wrapper around two plain HTML controls that already
 * work with no JavaScript at all:
 *
 *   - `<button type="submit" form={formId}>` — the `form` attribute lets this
 *     button submit `formId` even though it is not a descendant of that
 *     `<form>` (this bar is rendered after the form's own closing tag, sticky
 *     to the bottom of its section, not nested inside the form's markup). A
 *     real HTML form submission, exactly like a submit button written inside
 *     the form.
 *   - `<button type="reset" form={formId}>` (only when `resetLabel` is
 *     given) — same mechanism, native `reset` behaviour, no script.
 *
 * It introduces **no new endpoint**: whatever `formId` already submits to is
 * exactly what this bar triggers. `commerce-settings.astro`'s two settings
 * forms keep their own manual `submit` listeners and `sendJson` calls
 * unchanged — only the button that fires them moves outside the form, into
 * this component.
 *
 * ## Props
 *
 * | Prop         | Type      | Required | Notes                                                                |
 * | ------------ | --------- | -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
 * | `formId`     | `string`  | yes      | `id` of the `<form>` this bar's buttons submit/reset.                 |
 * | `saveLabel`  | `string`  | yes      | Primary button text — pass a translated string (`t("Save features")`).|
 * | `resetLabel` | `string`  | no       | Secondary button text. Omitted -> no secondary button is rendered.    |
 * | `ariaLabel`  | `string`  | no       | Landmark name. Defaults to `"Save changes"` (English fallback only — pass a translated value). |
 * | `class`      | `string`  | no       | Extra class name(s) merged onto the root, e.g. a screen-specific hook. |
 *
 * A named `status` slot carries the text beside the buttons — "Saved",
 * "Unsaved changes", or nothing at all. It is a slot rather than a string
 * prop so the caller can attach the `data-dirty-label`/`data-clean-label`
 * hooks the optional client half (below) reads, or render nothing.
 *
 * ## Demo
 *
 * ```astro
 * <section>
 *   <form id="commerce-features-form">
 *     ...fields...
 *   </form>
 *
 *   <CommerceSettingsSaveBar
 *     formId="commerce-features-form"
 *     saveLabel={t("Save features")}
 *     resetLabel={t("Reset")}
 *   >
 *     <span
 *       slot="status"
 *       id="commerce-features-save-status"
 *       data-dirty-label={t("Unsaved changes")}
 *       data-clean-label={t("Saved")}
 *     >
 *       {t("Saved")}
 *     </span>
 *   </CommerceSettingsSaveBar>
 * </section>
 * ```
 *
 * ## Always visible — no field-changed gate
 *
 * This bar renders UNCONDITIONALLY whenever the caller renders it at all
 * (subject only to the caller's own permission gate — see "Sticky within its
 * own section" below). A version that hides until a field changes would mean
 * a JavaScript-disabled visitor never sees a Save control at all on a screen
 * that treats this bar as its ONLY submit affordance — a real regression
 * given `AGENTS.md`'s "every core function works without JavaScript" rule.
 * The optional client module (`commerce-settings-save-bar-client.ts`) adds
 * only the dirty-vs-clean VISUAL distinction — a class toggle and an
 * optional status-text swap — never a visibility change.
 *
 * ## Sticky within its own section, not the whole page
 *
 * `position: sticky; bottom: 0` is scoped by the element's containing
 * block — the nearest block-container ancestor, e.g. the `<section>` this
 * bar's caller wraps its form (and this bar) inside. Placed there, the bar
 * sticks to the viewport bottom only while that section is still being
 * scrolled through, and releases once the section's own bottom edge comes
 * into view — so a page with TWO settings forms (like
 * `commerce-settings.astro`'s store-settings and features sections) never
 * shows two sticky bars overlapping at once. A caller that does NOT wrap its
 * form + bar in a shared block ancestor gets a bar that stays stuck for the
 * rest of the page instead — wrap them.
 *
 * ## Accessibility
 *
 * - `role="region"` + `aria-label` makes the bar a landmark a screen-reader
 *   user can jump to directly, separate from the form's own fields.
 * - Native buttons carry the browser's own focus/activation semantics; no
 *   custom focus ring is defined here — the bar's surface is
 *   `--color-surface` (a plain card, not a saturated fill), so the global
 *   `:focus-visible` rule in `tokens.css` already reads clearly on it.
 * - DOM order matters more than visual position for keyboard users: this bar
 *   is rendered where the caller places it (right after the form it
 *   submits), so Tab reaches it in the same order a sighted mouse user would
 *   expect, and `position: sticky` never changes tab order.
 * - 360px: actions wrap onto their own row, right-aligned, before the status
 *   text is squeezed under a comfortable touch height — see the `@media`
 *   block below.
 *
 * ## Buttons reuse the shared `.btn`/`.btn-primary` classes
 *
 * ADR-0120 already ships one button system (`admin.css`'s `.btn`,
 * `.btn-primary`, `.btn-secondary`) at 36px tall by deliberate choice for a
 * pointer-first admin (WCAG 2.2 AA 2.5.8 accepts 24px; see that file's own
 * comment). This bar's buttons use those classes rather than a bespoke
 * bar-specific button, so they look and behave exactly like every other
 * primary/secondary button pair on this page.
 */
interface Props {
  formId: string;
  saveLabel: string;
  resetLabel?: string;
  ariaLabel?: string;
  /** Extra class name(s) merged onto the root element. */
  class?: string;
}

const {
  formId,
  saveLabel,
  resetLabel,
  ariaLabel = "Save changes",
  class: className
} = Astro.props;

const hasStatusSlot = Astro.slots.has("status");
---

<div
  class:list={["commerce-save-bar", className]}
  role="region"
  aria-label={ariaLabel}
  data-commerce-save-bar
  data-commerce-save-bar-form={formId}
>
  {
    hasStatusSlot && (
      <div class="commerce-save-bar-status">
        <slot name="status" />
      </div>
    )
  }
  <div class="commerce-save-bar-actions">
    {
      resetLabel !== undefined && (
        <button type="reset" form={formId} class="btn commerce-save-bar-reset">
          {resetLabel}
        </button>
      )
    }
    <button
      type="submit"
      form={formId}
      class="btn-primary commerce-save-bar-save"
    >
      {saveLabel}
    </button>
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .commerce-save-bar {
    position: sticky;
    bottom: 0;
    z-index: var(--z-nav);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--sp-3);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: var(--sp-4);
    padding: var(--sp-3) var(--sp-4);
    /* iOS home-indicator safe area — the bar sits flush with the viewport
       bottom once stuck, and a device with a bottom gesture bar would
       otherwise render the buttons partly under it. */
    padding-bottom: calc(var(--sp-3) + env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px));
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-lg);
  }

  .commerce-save-bar-status {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-width: 0;
    font-size: var(--fs-sm);
    font-weight: var(--fw-semibold);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  /* Once a tracked form changes, `commerce-settings-save-bar-client.ts` adds
     this class — a colour shift only, never a visibility change (see the
     docblock above: the bar is never hidden). */
  .commerce-save-bar.is-dirty .commerce-save-bar-status {
    color: var(--color-primary);
  }

  .commerce-save-bar-actions {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--sp-2);
    flex: none;
    margin-left: auto;
  }

  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    /* Actions get their own row, right-aligned, before the status text is
       squeezed under a comfortable touch height. */
    .commerce-save-bar {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: stretch;
    }

    .commerce-save-bar-actions {
      justify-content: flex-end;
    }
  }
</style>
